  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nOur strategy improves upon the parent algorithm by adopting a binary representation for the function h(x), which is a common pattern for optimal solutions in similar problems. This allows the exploration of a larger solution space than the continuous representation. We use a binary step function with exactly half the domain set to 1 and half to 0. This reduces the problem to finding the best binary configuration.\n\nThe main bottleneck in the parent algorithm was the high dimensionality of the continuous representation. By switching to a binary representation with lower grid sizes (powers of two), we significantly reduce the number of variables, enabling faster optimization. We perform a local search by flipping bits (maintaining the sum constraint) and use multi-resolution by iterating over grid sizes from smallest to largest.\n\nWe use an optimized local search that flips two bits per trial (to preserve the integral constraint) and fixes dy resolution. This approach is computationally efficient and scalable.\n\nMathematical Specification\n1. **Objective Function**:\n   C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   Evaluated by: max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * (2.0/n_points)\n\n2. **Constraints**:\n   - Integral: \u2211h_i * (2/n) = 1 interpreted as exactly n/2 ones for binary vectors\n   - Bounds: h[i] \u2208 {0, 1} for all i\n\n3. **Representation**:\n   h(x) is binary, i.e., h[i] \u2208 {0,1} for all i. We represent it as a vector of zeros and ones with exactly n/2 ones.\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. **Input Handling**:\n   - Start `stopwatch` and set `best_global = (np.inf, None, None)`\n   - Predefine grid sizes: [16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512]\n\n2. **Multi-resolution Optimization**:\n   For each grid size n in grid_sizes:\n      - If remaining time < 0.5 seconds, break\n      - Generate an initial candidate: vector of zeros with n/2 random ones\n      - Perform local search:\n           1. Start with initial candidate\n           2. For each iteration:\n                 a. Generate new candidate by flipping two bits (one 0 to 1, one 1 to 0)\n                 b. Evaluate new candidate using compute_c5\n                 c. Update best candidate if new is better\n                 d. Number of iterations = 2 * ceil(log\u2082(n))\n      - Update global best candidate if current is better\n\n3. **Back to Structure**:\n   - After processing all grids within budget, return best candidate\n\nSearch Choices and Useful Variations\n1. **Grid Sizes**:\n   - Starts from smallest (16) to largest (512)\n   - Justification: Let smaller grids run longer to ensure better process\n\n2. **Mutation Operators**:\n   - We only flip two bits (0/1 pair) per trial\n   - This preserves the sum constraint\n\n3. **Termination Criteria**:\n   - Stops when either all grids processed or time exceeds budget\n\n4. **Adaptive Budget**:\n   - If n=16 takes t seconds, n=32 takes 2*t, n=64 takes 4*t, etc.\n\nComputational Budget and Robustness\n- Time complexity: O(n^2) for correlation, however N=512 is handled efficiently by FFT\n- n=16: ~0.0001s/evaluate\n- n=512: ~0.02s/evaluate\n- For each grid: ~2*log(n) evaluations\n- Total evaluations: 2 * log(512) \u2248 18 at game 512\n- Dominant cost: correlation, which is O(n^2) via FFT\n\nValidation and Return Contract\n1. Feasibility checks:\n   - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 (all hold by construction)\n   - \u2211h[i] == n/2 (preserved by bit flipping)\n\n2. Objective consistency:\n   - Recompute C5 and ensure matching\n\n3. Format:\n   - (1D numpy array, C5_value, n_points)\n\nThe above plan leverages the sparsity of optimal solutions while managing computational complexity through multi-resolution and local search.",
